1. Do these one at a time: argocd-grafana, harbour-vm, selfhosted-runner-1, selfhosted-runner-2.

2. On the control plane, drain it:
kubectl drain <worker-name> --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

3. On the worker itself:
sudo sed -i 's/v1\.31/v1.32/g' /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
sudo apt update

sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm && \
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm='1.32.x-*' && \
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

sudo kubeadm upgrade node

sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl && \
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet='1.32.x-*' kubectl='1.32.x-*' && \
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

4. Back on the control plane, uncordon it:

kubectl uncordon <worker-name>
kubectl get nodes

Wait until the worker shows Ready on v1.32.x before starting the next one.

5. Final check
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A

Before you drain any worker

Your earlier logs showed a node label argocd=true on argocd-grafana, and a CLOUD=true label on selfhosted-runner-2. Pods may be pinned to those nodes with a nodeSelector. If you drain a node and its pods can’t run anywhere else, they stay Pending until you uncordon it, which means downtime for that app.


