One heads-up: your container runtime is CRI-O

Your pods show cri-o://, not containerd. CRI-O is designed to run at the same minor version as Kubernetes. A one-version gap usually works, but check what you have on each worker:

crio --version

If the CRI-O package is installed from the same apt setup as Kubernetes, don’t upgrade it during this procedure unless you intend to. Just know which version you’re on. If anything breaks after a kubelet upgrade, the container runtime is one place to look.

1. A bare pod will stop the drain: dev/nginx

Its name has no random suffix, so it’s almost certainly a standalone pod with no Deployment behind it. kubectl drain refuses to evict those, and if you force it, the pod is deleted and never recreated.

